{"qid":"Q1794352","names":["Electorate of Trier"],"title":"Electorate of Trier","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Electorate of Trier was an ecclesiastical principality of the Holy Roman Empire ruled by the prince-archbishop of Trier, who held one of the seven electoral votes that chose the emperor. Its territory straddled the Moselle and middle Rhine, and under Archbishop Baldwin of Luxembourg (r. 1307-1354) it reached the height of its political weight, leveraging the family connection to Emperor Henry VII. Through the later medieval and early modern centuries the prince-archbishops balanced their dual roles as spiritual metropolitans and territorial princes, contesting authority with the city of Trier and the surrounding Rhenish powers.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is well documented through HRE cartography; borders shifted along the Moselle and Nahe valleys and are attested in multiple ecclesiastical and imperial surveys.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Electorate of Trier","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Balduin of Luxembourg","nativeName":"Balduin von Luxemburg","roman":"Balduin","start":1333,"end":1354,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Boemund II of Saarbrücken","nativeName":"Boemund II. von Saarbrücken","roman":"Boemund II","start":1354,"end":1362,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Kuno II of Falkenstein","nativeName":"Kuno II. von Falkenstein","roman":"Kuno II","start":1362,"end":1388,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Werner of Fal